From: David Bau <davidbau@csail.mit.edu>
Date: 2026-06-19
Subject: [PATCH] init_level: read parsed .align field, not bit-colliding .flags

`init_level()` derives a level's fallback alignment from the
dungeon by extracting bits 4-6 of `tmpdungeon[dgn].flags & D_ALIGN_MASK`.
The `UNCONNECTED` flag (0x10, in `dgn_file.h:60`) occupies the same bit
position as `D_ALIGN_CHAOTIC` (`AM_CHAOTIC << 4 = 0x10`, in
`dgn_file.h:63`). Any dungeon with the `UNCONNECTED` flag is therefore
silently treated as chaotic-aligned by the fallback path.

`struct tmpdungeon` already has a separately-parsed `align` field
(`dgn_file.h:26`) which the Lua loader correctly populates at
`dungeon.c:1057` from the `alignment = "..."` table key. Reading that
field — not re-extracting from `.flags` — avoids the collision.

The visible effect today: the Tutorial dungeon (the only stock dungeon
with `UNCONNECTED` set but no `alignment` key) silently inherits
`AM_CHAOTIC`, and `align_shift()` (`makemon.c:1611`) returns +2 for
most difficulty-1 monsters under that alignment, biasing the
Tutorial's monster spawn weights uniformly upward.

Fix: replace the `.flags` re-extraction with a read of the
already-parsed `.align` field, shifted into the raw AM_* layout
`flags.align` expects.

---
 src/dungeon.c | 3 +--
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/dungeon.c b/src/dungeon.c
index 0000000..0000001 100644
--- a/src/dungeon.c
+++ b/src/dungeon.c
@@ -587,8 +587,7 @@ init_level(int dgn, int proto_index, struct proto_dungeon *pd)

     new_level->flags.align = ((tlevel->flags & D_ALIGN_MASK) >> 4);
     if (!new_level->flags.align)
-        new_level->flags.align =
-            ((pd->tmpdungeon[dgn].flags & D_ALIGN_MASK) >> 4);
+        new_level->flags.align = pd->tmpdungeon[dgn].align >> 4;

     new_level->rndlevs = tlevel->rndlevs;
     new_level->next = (s_level *) 0;
